In article <3nju8c$dq0@balsam.unca.edu>, 
brock@cs.unca.edu (J. Dean Brock) writes...

> I just got an AlphaStation 200 4/100 with no monitor.
> I connected a terminal to the serial port ...
> maybe someone out there could answer the following questions
> 
>	1)  What baud rate do you use?
>	2)  Which of the two serial ports should be used as the console?

    This response holds for the AlphaStation 200, 250, and 400.

    At power-on, the console prompt is displayed on both the graphics
    monitor (if it and a keyboard are present) and a terminal connected to
    the COM1 serial port.  To ensure that all console traffic is directed
    to the serial port, you have to type the console command "set console
    serial";  alternately, "set console graphics" will cause the console to
    use the keyboard and graphics adapter hardware.

    This feature allows use of a serial console even when a keyboard and
    graphics adapter are present;  this is handy, for instance, to log the
    startup messages on another device.

    For those of you who are thinking "Gee, why don't they force the serial
    console when no keyboard or graphics adapter is detected?", that
    feature is not provided.

    I believe that a terminal attached to the serial console must be set
    for

    		9600 BAUD (receive and transmit)
    		full duplex
    		eight bits
    		no parity
    		one stop bit

    The COM1 device connector is a male, nine-pin D-subminiature connector. 
    You'll need a female cable connector, or adapter to hook to it.  On the
    AlphaStation 400, it's the one on the right as you look at the back of
    the box.  On the AlphaStation 200 and 250, it is the one on top.  The
    pinout is shown below.

              signal   pin

              DCD       1
              RX        2
              TX        3                 1 2 3 4 5
              DTR       4               .-----------.
              gnd       5               \ � � � � � /   looking into jack
              DSR       6                \ � � � � /    on the device
              RTS       7                 ---------     enclosure
              CTS       8                  6 7 8 9
              RI        9

    I've been told that, if you want to use DECconnect, the adapter is
    H8571-J, and the DECconnect (linguini) cable is BC16E-10.


    Regarding the missing page numbers:  I'm told this was a slip-up in
    demand printing at the manufacturing site -- manuals without page
    numbers were supposed to go only to my engineering team.  Allegedly
    fixed.

    -- Kenny

