In article <3nln7i$h1p@atglab10.atglab.bls.com>, Mike Richards <mike.richards@bst.bls.com> writes:
> Is there a compression utility offered by DEC or a third party that is
> licensed to run on a VAX?  I say "licensed" because ZOO is preferred
> by the user, but ZOO, to my understanding is shareware and my company
> won't allow shareware to be run on production boxes.
> 
> Thanks in advance.     
> 
> -Beau Hardeman
> 
> 
> 
> 
-- 
Beau,
 
Check into the LIBRARIAN utility, it does have the capability of creating 
compressed text libraries.
 
A second alternative, is to carefully review your organization's policies. 
In many organizations, the prohibition on "shareware" refers to executable 
binaries obtained through "unknown" means. If your organization has this 
flavor of restriction, you have two options which will likely work:
 
 1 - Obtain the (available) sources for ZIP/UNZIP from a major FTP site 
     (FTP.SPC.EDU and WKUVX1.WKU.EDU come to mind), examine the sources, 
     and then compile and link them at your site.
 
 2 - Obtain the binaries from a reasonably reliable and documented source.
     In the case of ZIP/UNZIP, the sources and executables are on the
     FREEWARET62 CDROM distributed by Digital at the last DECUS. Since 
     several hundred (if not thousands) of these disks were distributed, 
     with apparently no problem, it should be ok.
 
It is important to note that most shareware restrictions are phrased to 
avoid PC dangers. Public domain sources of major programs are often exempt 
(or issued waivers), since the sources are available and can be maintained 
locally if need me.
 
I hope that the above is helpful. If I have been unclear, or can be of 
further assistance, please feel free to contact me via Email or phone.
 
