In article <D7q02J.KMM@irvine.com>, jsweet@irvine.com (Jerry Sweet) writes:

>To be fair, it must be said that just about any piece of commercial
>software that runs in a multiuser environment seems to require some
>kind of wacko license scheme.  However, I do find it puzzling as to
>how it can do anything but work to the detriment of hardware vendors
>to make their own enabling software expensive and painful both to
>deploy and to update.

Would you rather we raise the price of the hardware so that the compilers
(which are expensive to develop and maintain) are funded?  TANSTAAFL.
What about users who don't want C but want Fortran instead?  Why should
they pay higher prices for a "free" C compiler?

Licensing schemes are there mainly because computer software is so
incredibly easy to "pirate".  Note that Digital and other vendors have moved
from having only system-wide (expensive) licenses to much cheaper
user-based licensing.  To administer this properly, a licensing scheme is
needed.  If you didn't have licensing software, you'd have no
user-based licensing, no consolidated distribution CD and high license
fees.

There is very little margin in hardware nowadays - it doesn't support the
investment in compiler and other software technology.  If you want a C
or Fortran or Ada compiler, you pay for it. 

