Developing a compiler costs the vendor.

Since "C" is not the language for me, I prefer not to have the cost
a "C" compiler for your benefit bundled into the cost of an operating
system which I might buy.

I would not extend this attitude to the absurd level of individual
commands like Backup or Copy, but at the level of a language compiler
we are talking about something which is a large enough cost item and
sufficiently a matter of personal (or organization) taste that the
unbunded approach becomes appropriate.

As for "commercial software developers", I think we are the group
most likely to perceive compiler purchase as adding value.  There
are several DEC compiler products, in fact, where different "levels"
of support have been made available for the same language. In the
one I have looked at most closely, the pricing is quite fair (that is,
the choice of whether or not to spend the extra amount for the extra
features is really a close call).

