In article <3nm98u$reo@solaris.cc.vt.edu>, marchany@vtserf.cc.vt.edu (Randy Marchany) writes:
>I have a user that has an Alpha 200 4/166 and he needs to boot it in
>single user mode (OSF). There is no halt button in the back of the unit
>and the red init button starts the boot process to multiuser mode. He
>has a problem with his startup code and consequently can't shutdown with
>the halt option. How do we get into single user mode so we can change
>the boot_osflags?  Thanks for any help.

Can you shutdown (without the -h) at all?  If so, this should get you to single
user mode.

Failing that, you might be able to do some quickly typed ^C characters during
the boot sequence (before osfboot comes up).  Failing that, there's always the
possibility of removing the boot device.  When it fails to find the boot
device, I'm quite sure it'll stop at console problem.  

Once at the console prompt:

	>>> setenv boot_osflags s

or

	>>> boot -flag s 

should get you into single user mode.

I don't remember if the 200s have it, but the 400s have a jumper on the cpu
module that will cause the cpu to halt to the console if you push the front
panel button.  The jumper comes from the factory in the "reset and start all
over" position.

