In article <1995Apr25.175938.24679@eos.arc.nasa.gov>,
maclure@eos.arc.nasa.gov (IanMaclure) wrote:

> On a multi-processor AXP ( 2100? ) ( under OpenVMS and OSF1 ) is 
> it possible to specify that a given process be loaded into a 
> predetermined CPU and remain there for the life of the process. 
> I am definitely not interested in SMP as the processes are NOT
> in general symmetric.

I'm not sure what you mean by processes not being symmetric, but
one of the benefits of SMP is being able to dynamically schedule a
process on any processor.

However, for Digital UNIX:

runon(1)
SYNOPSIS

  runon cpu_num command [command_args]

DESCRIPTION

  The runon utility runs command on CPU number cpu_num.  The command_args
  parameter indicates flags or arguments, if any, that are specified for com-
  mand.  All threads created by command will inherit the CPU binding cpu_num.
  This includes threads created as the result of a fork.

bind_to_cpu(3)
SYNOPSIS

  #include <sys/types.h>
  #include <sys/resource.h>

  int bind_to_cpu(
          pid_t pid,
          unsigned long cpu_mask ,
          unsigned long flag );

DESCRIPTION

  Upon return from bind_to_cpu, all threads of the target pid are running on
  the target CPU.  Bound threads are not eligible for execution on any other
  CPU. You release CPU binding by invoking bind_to_cpu with a  cpu_mask of
  zero.

And if you use threads:

pthread_bind_to_cpu_np(3)
SYNOPSIS

  #include <pthread.h>
  int pthread_bind_to_cpu_np(
       pthread_t *thread ,
       unsigned long *cpu_mask );

DESCRIPTION

  This routine binds a thread to a particular processor on a multiprocessor
  system.  Once bound, a thread will only run on the specified CPU until the
  thread terminates, or the binding is changed.

  Specify a cpu_mask of 0 to allow a previously bound thread to execute on
  any available CPU.

