In article <3nh7ad$c9k@prop.wes.army.mil>, heath@hl.wes.army.mil (Ronnie Heath) writes:
> One problem is that DEC (along with SGI, HP, etc.) spend a lot of money supporting 
> their flavor of Unix.  Alpha workstations loaded with OSF or VMS cost $3,000+ more 
> than the same box with Windows/NT. [...]
> The only hope for lower pricing on Unix is a port of Linux 
> to the Alpha.

But that's not true. You can buy an NT AlphaStation and a VMS or OSF/1 license
for it on the side for less money than the official VMS-only (or OSF/1 only)
package. What happened is that when they came up with the AlphaStation, some
manager had to decide how much to sell it for. The VMS and OSF/1 managers
looked up the prices of the machines that were available then and followed the
usual workstation equation that the price remains about the same but you get
more performance for that price. The result is a price that makes business
sense but does not necessarily have anything to do with production costs. The
NT department probably compared to DEC's high-end PCs and set the price
accordingly. This has nothing to do with the development costs for VMS or
OSF/1, it has to do with charging as much as you can. They charge less for NT
because NT systems compete directly with Pentium systems which are actually
quite fast for typical networking/integer applications. You can't run VMS on a
PC and typical DEC customers aren't interested in running Linux on a PC either,
so the VMS and OSF/1 systems compete with workstations which are more
expensive.

DEC has a very cheap NT system, the Multia. It's got too little memory to be of
much use, though, and the disk isn't fast either. I heard on the grapevine that
there will be a Multia-2 that will also run VMS and OSF/1. To avoid killing
their market they'll have to find a way to limit its usefulness so it doesn't
replace the more expensive workstations. I have no idea what they'll do, but
I'm sure they'll find something :-) Or rather, :-(

A long time ago, IBM had an entry-level tape unit called the 3410. This came in
3 models, each faster than the preceding one. When you ordered an upgrade from
the -1 to the -2, an engineer came over, gave you a generous handshake, opened
the cover, inserted a jumper, closed the cover, and wished you a nice day (the
-3 upgrade actually included a new motor and high-current recording head). The
difference in production cost between the -1 and -2 was zero, and yet the -2
was twice the speed and 50% more expensive. It made business sense to many
customers to pay for the upgrade, because it saved them money. The two units,
however, were identical. IBM could not provide the -2 performance at the price
of the -1 without losing revenue. Customers were content to pay traditional
prices for the traditional tape units, and next year they'd buy the new model
that went 50% faster for the same price, but if you offered them the same thing
for 30% less, they'd pay 30% less of course. The -1 was targeted at shops that
needed a small tape unit for software installation and that used removable
disks for backups. IBM told its engineers to design that and when they came
back with a cheap unit that was actually quite close to the traditional ones,
they were told to cripple it so it could be sold for what it was supposed to
be. So they used a slower motor with a jumper to further divide the speed by 2.
This only worked because noone knew about it. We found out through a friend and
upgraded our -1 to -2 ourselves, and since we owned the hardware and the
contract never said certain jumpers had to be in certain positions, IBM
couldn't do anything about it as long as we didn't publicize our findings.

  Eric



