In article <3npi8l$8k0@usenet.rpi.edu>,
Wesley Kaplow <kaploww@cs.rpi.edu> wrote:
>I am the proud new owner of a DECStation 5000/200.  Unfortunately,
>it only has a ThinNet Ethernet connection, which is a realy problem in
>my office.   Without re-wiring my office to put in a ThinWire segment,
>the only option is to obtain a ThickWire (read transciever interface)
>expansion board.
>
>Please Emial offers to kaploww@cs.rpi.edu

You never actually mention what the wiring situation in your office
is - I'm assuming its 10 Base T..  If so, buy the cheapest 10BT
repeater you can find w/an AUI (Thickwire) port.  Get a Thinwire/AUI
transceiver, 2 T connectors, 2 terminators, and a wire segment.  Hook
the transceiver into the aui port on the repeater & run a thinwire
segment from it to the tap on the DECstation.  Then buy or build a
10BT crossover cable & plug the entire mess into the wall with that.

ie: (+ is a T connector, o is a terminator)

	DS5k	
	 |  10B2 segmnt
	o+--------------+o
			X  10B2 transceiver
		|------------|	
		| repeater   |
		 ------------
			|
			| 10Bt Crossoever cable
			|
------------------------------------  Wall


We did this to move 3 DS5000/200s when we retired our thinnet
network. We had plenty of spare thinnet stuff laying around, so this
was quite cheap for us.  If you don't have similar spare parts, then
you might be better off getting the card.




