From: gordon_b@apollo.HP.COM (Bruce Gordon)
Subject: Re: 32-Bit floating point format
Date: 1995/04/25
Message-ID: <D7LExo.rI@apollo.hp.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 101473442
sender: usenet@apollo.hp.com (Usenet News)
references: <3niq25$pri@cd4680fs.rrze.uni-erlangen.de>
organization: Hewlett-Packard Company, Chelmsford, MA
keywords: floating point
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <3niq25$pri@cd4680fs.rrze.uni-erlangen.de>, tsschuel@informatik.uni-erlangen.de (Thomas Schueller) writes:
|> Hello !
|> 
|> Does somebody out there know how the 32-bit floating point used by a Vax
|> looks like ?
|> I need this information in order to convert some floating-numbers into
|> another number-format.
|> 
|> Thanks in advance,
|> 
|> Thomas Schueller
|> 
  3                       1
  1                       6
 +-------------------------+
 | Fraction                |
 +-------------------------+
   1  1               
   5  4        7 6        0 
 +---+----------+----------+
 | S | Exponent | Fraction |
 +---+----------+----------+

bit 31 is the sign bit
bits 23 - 30 are the exponent in excess 128 format (i.e. an exponent
of 0 is 2^-128, an exponent of 128 is 2^0, and an exponent of 255 is
2^127)
the rest of the bits are the frational part and are always assumed to
begin with 0.1.  This bit is known as the phantom bit, it is always
set but you can't see it.

Examples:
                              Sign  Exponent  Fration
	 5.0 is 000041A0 hex    0   10000011  [.1]01000000000000000000000
	 2.0 is 00004100 hex    0   10000010  [.1]00000000000000000000000
	 1.0 is 00004080 hex    0   10000001  [.1]00000000000000000000000
	 0.5 is 00004000 hex    0   10000000  [.1]00000000000000000000000
	-1.0 is 0000C080 hex    1   10000001  [.1]00000000000000000000000

Note: I put in the leading bit but the vax does not.  If you count all
the bits, including the phantom bit, you'll see there are actually 33
bits.

This information can be found in the VAX Architecture Reference
Manual.

Bruce

