Alpha machine?  What alpha machine?  I was comparing DECstations with
Pentium boxes.  As it happens, I have an AlphaStation to work with too,
and it also performs with 40% of what I'd expect based on specFP.

I'm running a "real world ap" of my own which uses memory >> cache, 
but which has good locality--however, it spans the entire data set
every timestep (meaning EVERYTHING has to be fetched from memory
and then written.)

Both the AlphaStation I was using and the Pentium used 512k of
cache.

Oh, and the code on the Alpha was compiled with gcc too, same version
and same flags.

You seem to have these hypothetical real-world applications in mind
that you claim makes a huge difference but I'm actually running
"real world applications" and I find that the spec numbers
are good to within 40%, CERTAINLY much better than within factors of 3-4.

My only claim is that the following spec numbers show that
a pentium "blows the doors off" a dec 5000/20:

System       CPU       ClkMHz Cache        SPEC  SPEC  Info  Source
Name         Type      ext/in Ext+I/D      92Int 92 FP Date  Obtained

DEC 5020     R3000     20     64/64         13.5  18.4 Jun93 DECinfo
Intel Xpress Pentium   ??/90  512+8/8      106.5  81.4 Mar95 www.intel.com
DEC 5133     R3000     33     64/128        20.9  29.1 Jun93 DECinfo

Note the factor of 4 difference between the pentium and the 5000/20 in
specFP, and the factor of 2.8 between the pentium and the 5000/133.

I don't think you'd even comment if I said that a 5000/133 "blows
the doors off" a 5000/20.  If you can come up with a non-cooked-up
"real world floating point ap" which runs faster (or even
comparably fast)  on the 5000/20 than a pentium, then tell me.  
I think you will look long.

Here is an example of two spec numbers which don't give a clear indication
of which machine is superior.

DEC 3500     A21064    30/150 512+8/8       84.4 127.7 Apr93 comp.arch(DEC)
HP 715/64    PA7100LC  64     256           66.6  96.5 Aug94 www.hp.com

Here you'd be well advised to try your program on each machine to see
which runs it faster.  

Regards,

PeterM



