The situation:

We have been using MH (via xmh and mh-e) on an Alpha running OSF/1 for
several months with no problem. We have used the binaries that came
with the system (in /usr/bin/mh).  Until a couple of days ago our home
directories were on the same Alpha as the heaviest user of MH.  We
just installed a new SGI Challenge server and have moved our user home
directories to that machine.  So now the Alpha mounts the user
directories using NFS.  Since the change, anno goes into a suspended
state every time it is invoked by mh-e or xmh.  So, for instance, if
you reply to mail with the "a" command of mh-e, everything is peachy
except that the "sending..." message in the minibuffer never goes away
and emacs seizes up.  You have to ^G and then kill the draft
buffer. The mail is sent and Fcced properly, but the original message
is not annotated.  A ps shows anno hovering in the background.  Xmh
exhibits very similar behavior.  I have rebuilt MH from source and
still have the same problem.

Smells to me like a problem with NFS mounts from SGI to DEC, perhaps
related to file locking.  Unfortunately, this is exactly the kind of
bug that neither SGI nor DEC is gonna help squash ("We don't support
MH.").  Anybody got a clue?

T'anks.

