In article <dliebson.227.01A28E4E@mit.edu> dliebson@mit.edu (David M Liebson) writes:
> I'm looking for the specifications for the interface to these drives (I've 
> been told they have the same interface.)  I gather that the interface is some
> mutant sort of SCSI-- I'd like to know how it differs.

The CM-100 and CM-201 use an interface that is nothing like SCSI.  It has
one serial line from the host adapter to the drive, and four serial lines from
the drive to the host adapter.  The CM-100 uses RS-422 differential signals,
and the CM-201 uses TTL levels.

The signals are:

Command   host to drive, 19.2 Kbps async serial, 8 bits Odd parity 1 stop
Response  drive to host, 19.2 Kbps async serial, 8 bits Odd parity 1 stop bit
Clock     drive to host, 3.0 Mbps
Data      drive to host, 3.0 Mbps synchronous, valid on rising edge of clock
Attention drive to host, data error flag, asserted for each byte in error

The drive doesn't do the layer 3 error correction; the host adapter or host
software is expected to do it!

Someone at Phillips in Europe was kind enough to send me the CM-100 user
manual and the service anuals for the CM-100 and CM-110 drives and the CM-153
host adapter.  The CM-100 user manual describes the interface to the drive,
but there isn't enough detail to actually build a working host adapter or
driver.

The CM-110 is a SCSI version of the CM-100.  It contains the complete CM-100
electronics plus a SCSI interface board.  I'd very much like to get one of
these (either the complete drive or just the SCSI interface board).

I'd also like to get a KRQ50 Q-Bus host adapter if anyone has a spare.

Cheers,
Eric



