As an example of how workstation are not all they are cracked up to be.
I do Protein modeling and use mt own code.  I found my new 100MHz
Pentium ran the simulations at 2/3 the speed of what I thought
was a 275MHz Alpha.  I did not balaive this could be true.  So
I checked into it and found the Alpha was 200MHz (still fast and
expensive).  So OK but I thought a 200MHz Alpha should kill a 
Pentium the SpecFp is still 3 times as high.  So I got the Spec
list and found out what they test and how several things scored.
To my amazement the Pentiums did really well in the integer tests
actually being the fastest at the gcc compile.  They did not do 
as well in the fp section but as fast as half as fast as the 275MHz
Alpha in one test.

So while i was on this ftp site I grabbed clinpack.c.  I compiled 
is on the 200MHz Alpha and got 21 double presision MFlops, the
Pentium got 12 double presision MFlops.  

Now my code is all double float stuff but there is a lot of 
bookkeeping so the times are 1.2 sec for a small test on the alpha
and 1.7 sec for the same test on the Pentium.  A little better
than the linpack test just like I expaected (after I looked int it)

Based on my experience unless you Really need your answers in 5sec
instead of ten there rally is no need for a $20,000 workstation.
I would hope the workstation vendors see the writing on the wall
and start offering real systems in the $5000 range instead of 
those crap Indys with R4600 and Suns with MicroSparc chips.
A 100MHz Pentium with Linux is not a bad alturnative.  I am 
curious about a 133MHz PPC604 machine running Linux would be 
like.  Not too bad I would suppose.  Or a 133MHz P6,   I think
Sun and HP should reconsider their marketing strateges.

Bill

