From: fairfield@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu
Subject: Re: VMS Help libraries
Date: 1995/04/23
Message-ID: <1995Apr22.193733.1@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 101360629
sender: news@unixhub.SLAC.Stanford.EDU
references: <3nbk79$bka@bingnet1.cc.binghamton.edu>
organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <3nbk79$bka@bingnet1.cc.binghamton.edu>, 
    	consp01@bingsuns.cc.binghamton.edu (Lars Kellogg-Stedman) writes:
> I want to take a VMS help library and decompile it back into its 
> text-only representation.
> 
> How do I do this?  The eventual goal is to convert a small help file into 
> a series of web pages.

        There's no "decompile" required.  Simply use the LIBRARY command
    with the /EXTRACT  qualifer  to  extract  individual  "modules" into
    plain ASCII text files.  Start with a /LIST of the library, as in,

        $ LIB/LIST SYS$HELP:HELPLIB.HLB
    or
    	$ LIB/HELP/LIS SYS$HELP:HELPLIB

    to see the names of the  modules  in the library.  Next, extract the
    one you want:

    	$ LIB/HELP/EXTR=COPY/OUT=COPY.HLP HELPLIB

    This example produces the text file COPY.HLP.  

        Formatting the  result  for  useful  WEB  pages  may  be another
    matter.   Note only that the implicit hierarchy of the help  topics,
    subtopics, sub-subtopics, etc., is signified by a character 1, 2, 3,
    etc., in column 1 of the text file.

        Having said all that (prompted  mainly by the word "decomplie"),
    you  may find it easier to use the HELP command's /OUTPUT  qualifer.
    For example, to get the full text of COPY and its  subtopics,  etc.,
    you  could do the following (note that the ellipses are required, as
    shown, to get the main topic and its subtopics):

        $ HELP/OUT=COPY.TXT COPY...

    In this case, each topic  and  subtopic  is formatted with the topic
    header,  subtopic  header, sub-subtopic header, etc., in the  output
    file.  That may or may not be easier to deal with than the .HLP file
    itself.

        Finally, in case the point  has  otherwise  been missed, you can
    specify  your user-written help library to the HELP command by using
    the /LIBRARY  qualifier  (not  to  be  confused  with  the  /LIBLIST
    qualifier):

        $ HELP/LIBR=MY_DIR:MY_HELP/OUT=NEATO.TXT NEATO...

            -Ken
