    			Jim Simpson <jds@pc5.hep.anl.gov> writes:
> Our experimental research group has an lp daemon running
> on a PC at a remote location.  I am seeking software to run
> on a VAX 3800 (with MultiNet) which will provide Unix-like
> lpr service to a remote node without having to define
> a permanent printer queue on the VAX.

        I hate to  say  the  obvious,  but  "why  not"?  I've found that
    configuring  remote printer queues under Multinet is extremely  easy
    and "just works".  It's as easy to delete them as to  add  them,  if
    for  some  reason  you  don't  want them to be "permanently".  Since
    Multinet has already done the work for you, why not use it??

> The remote printer is PostScript, so sotfware which can
> package text into PostScript wrapping (when requested)
> would be nice, but not a critical requirement.

        Depending on the exact requirements,  a  SETUP module may do the
    trick.   If you want to modify the print symbiont, that's a bit more
    work.

        My  experience  with   both   PC-attached   printers,  and  (the
    wonderful!)  HP  Laserjet 4M-Plus directly on the Ethernet, is  that
    you can simply define two separate queues, one  for  text,  one  for
    postscript,  and point the Multinet lpr queues at PC-attached queues
    that expect plain text or PostScript, respectively.

        -Ken
