In article <3n94m4$1b61@te6000.otc.lsu.edu>, "Alaric S. Haag" <haag@imr00.me.lsu.edu> writes:
> Hello all!
> 
> I have a VAX 4000/300 running VMS V5.5 with 3 DSSI RF31 drives. This
> gives me about 1 Gb of storage and it's high time to add more!! The
> question is; would it be better to swap out for a high-capacity DSSI
> drive, or add a SCSI controller (QBUS) and get SCSI drive(s)? In either
> case, are there specific drives/controllers to aim for/stay away from?
> 
> Any info, suggestions, random ats of kindness, etc will be greatly 
> appreciated!!

You should deal with SCSI drives.  You can add controllers to
either your DSSI bus (a dssi-scsi adaptor) or add a controller to
your Q bus (a Qbus-scsi adaptor).  Both are available from CMD
Technologies and both can be found on the used market relatively
inexpensively.

New:    CMD Cobra (dssi-scsi)
        CQD 4xx (qbus-scsi)

Used:   CDI-4000 (dssi-scsi)
        CQD 2xx (qbus-scsi)

Cobra/CDI allows you to add up to 7 drives/controller to your
DSSI bus and up to 7 contollers to the bus.  CQD allows you to
add up to 7 drives/controller.  CQD allows tape to be added as
well, I'm not sure if Cobra allows this.

Great products.  I was just at an auction and ran into a reseller
claiming to have a bunch of factory refurbished CMD product
including CDI-4000.  His number:

        Matt Sousa
        Computer Peripheral Resources
        800.775.8273

This isn't a recommendation, just a pointer to someone who has
what you might need.
