In article <arneznevy99bdrgy_-_@hodgkin.mbi.ucla.edu>
arne@hodgkin.mbi.ucla.edu (Arne Elofsson) wrote:

> 
> But can anyone tell my why the same Dec machine with a pentium is about
> $4000 cheaper than the same machine with a 166 MHz alpha (21064).
> The price of the CPU differs only marginally. The extra chips can not
> cost that much. My guess is that Dec just are plain stupid. If they
> were releasing $5000 alphas and $10000 21164 running NT or for the
> ones of ous that want OSF. They would kill the high-end clone business.
> 
> Any other thoughts ?
> 
One problem is that DEC (along with SGI, HP, etc.) spend a lot of money supporting 
their flavor of Unix.  Alpha workstations loaded with OSF or VMS cost $3,000+ more 
than the same box with Windows/NT.  If you compare discounted pricing on roughly 
equivalent systems (cache,  memory, graphics, etc.) running NT, the Alpha is 
competitive on price/performance.

You can save a little money by buying Alpha systems from clone makers such as 
Aspen and BTG.  If the clones can establish a market, pricing on NT systems should 
become more competitive.  The only hope for lower pricing on Unix is a port of Linux 
to the Alpha.  Of course, if you want to crunch numbers on a Alpha desktop, NT is just 
as fast as Unix and cheaper.

Ronnie Heath
heath@hl.wes.army.mil




