organization: HeadCandy Associates... Sweets for the lobes.
newsgroups: _swat.announce.cc,_trico.computing.sysadmins,comp.unix.pc-clone.32bit,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.intel,comp.benchmarks

In article <3mkrhi$88l@larch.cc.swarthmore.edu> sasha@raptor.sccs.swarthmore.edu (Alexandre Vorobiev) writes:

   Can anyone comment on the following performance comparison:
   DEC 5000/133 runnning Ultrix 4.2A vs. Pentium 100 running Solaris or Linux.
   The station would be primarily used as a newshost, webserver, and 
   a dialup login server.
   Would such a trade (DECstation for a Pentium machine) be worthwhile?
   Any other comments or suggestions would be appreciated.

My 486DX2/80 is roughly the same speed in general benchmarking as the
DECstation 5000/25 I used to have on my desk at my previous job.  The
PC is a bit more responsive in personal use than the DECstation.  A
Pentium would definitely give you better general-purpose performance
than a DEC 5000/133.

The qualifier here is that even a DEC 5000/25 will blow the doors off
an Intel chip (even a P6) in floating-point performance.  If you need
to do serious number crunching, the RISC workstations are better,
hands-down.

But for a network server, that Pentium (with a PCI bus and PCI SCSI
controller) should do a fine job.

My only point of contention would be running Linux as a network
server.  Linux may be a fine operating system to do interesting things
on, but its weakest point is networking.

On the other hand, NetBSD and FreeBSD have networking as solid as any
unix available.  I would definitely encourage you to consider one of
them.  I would definitely not encourage Linux use for a heavy-duty
network server.

