Caveat: I come from an SGI world.  Entering the DEC universe has been
a painful experience.  The machines are fast, but expensive.  The
worst part has been how DEC mouths "open systems" with one tongue,
while shipping closed systems that create unnecessary headaches for
customers.  That's why I'm writing.  DEC executives, listen up.

DEC still believes in software hobbling (i.e., copy protection),
something the desktop PC market gave up years ago.  Customer service
is, I'm happy to say, very good when they know what they're talking
about.  BUT, they proved unable to help me with my PAK authorization
problems.  Only on the fifty-somethingth try was I able to get the
PAK to "take".  This is not a typing error, folks -- I have been
re-entering the same data time and time again.  With SGI, I just plug
in my CD-ROM, power on the machine, install, and go -- if SGI hasn't
done this for me!  No license PAKs to screw up, no crippled logins to
contend with.

The only reason we're using DECs is because of the industry we're in
(transportation and energy management), which has a historical
preference for DEC products.  That is to say, our customers tell *us*
what platforms they want to use.  However, that's changing.  DEC
hasn't been at the top of the bang/buck heap in years.  This is what
customers want...  along with *true* systems openness.  DEC would,
based on my interactions with them, just as soon you bought incredibly
expensive and probably unique-to-DEC peripherals.

A good example of DEC's "buy it here or do without" philosophy: I have
an Archive Python 4350XT 4mm DAT drive for backups.  On the DEC
3000/300, I have to manually dork the kernal config files, rebuild the
kernel, and reboot the system before I can use it.  This means that I
can't use the Python if I have a major system crash requiring a
complete rebuild from scratch.  Naturally, DEC wouldn't help me when I
ran into problems making this play.  On the SGI Indy (or any other SGI
machine), I plug the Python into the thing's SCSI tailpipe, and reboot
the machine.  Not only does the Indy recognize the drive right away,
it builds the /dev nodes I need, including soft links for /dev/tape.

DEC can hide behind their existing customer base just so long... but
the natives are restless.
-- 
