From: Ollie Treadway <olliet@nando.net>
Subject: Update on Alphastation as a C++ compiler (compared to Pentium)
Date: 1995/04/21
Message-ID: <3n78db$n0r@parsifal.nando.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 101268588
references: <3n44ft$5u4@itchy.itsnet.com>
content-type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
organization: News & Observer Public Access
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1b3 (Windows; I; 32bit)

This is an update to a previous posting on this topic.  With the tremendous  
help of the Digital Alpha ISV team and Matt LaPine of the Digital Alpha NT Visual   
C++ team we discovered the problem that was causing our compile times to be so bad   
on the AlphaStation. 
 
In order to have a common set of make files to build our system on a number of  
different machines, we defined the path for source files as M:\... 
where M: may be mapped to a local drive or a remote drive depending on where the 
build was being done.  This did not generate a great deal of overhead on our 
intel development machines.  However, mapping a local drive to M: on the  
AlphaStation was causing each compile to take several minutes. 
 
After changing the path to use the local drive letter, our compiles started  
running in a few seconds rather than a few minutes.  
 
Based on the compile times I observed after making the above change, the   
AlphaStation looks very good compared to the Pentium. 
 
Also, the high quality of support I received today from Digital is another   
compelling reason for using the AlphaStation as a development platform. 
 
Ollie Treadway 
olliet@nando.net




