In article <3n6cs8$hqn@amhux3.amherst.edu>,
pdchapin@news.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) wrote:

> What do I gain with DECAthena as opposed to just running NIS/NFS?

You get a central administration point for users, file services,
passwords, etc. You add a user once, then that user can log on anywhere
without having to update /etc/passwd or automount or anything.
The administration utility is Motif, but you can use a command line
if you want.

You get easy workstation installation. It takes <15 minutes from
opening the box and taking out your new workstation, to logging on.
If you accidentally rm /vmunix, or you suspect somebody has done
something dodgy, or just for the heck of it, reinstall. It takes
hardly any time at all.

You get security. The whole environment uses Kerberos for authentication.
You can quite freely tell everyone what the workstation root password is,
and it won't help them one bit. Sure they can hack their own workstation,
but who cares? (Apparently MIT has the root password written in foot-high
letters on a wall somewhere.)

You get management savings. The estimates are something like 1 manager
per 20 workstations in a normal environment, 1 manager per 200 workstations
in an Athena environment (from memory).

You get a service, not a software product. Digital sells DECAthena as
a consulting project, not a CD. This means we come to you and set it
up for you.

You get added extras like Zephyr for "real-time" messaging, on-line
conferencing, and a couple of others I can't remember at the moment.

You get easy upgrades. Instead of running around n workstations, and
upgrading each one, you can do a central upgrade and let the workstations
upgrade themselves.

That's all I can dredge up from memory. Try doing a search on
http://www.digital.com/ for DECAthena.

PJDM
