Peter Mayne (Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com) wrote:
: In article <3n6cs8$hqn@amhux3.amherst.edu>,
: pdchapin@news.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) wrote:

: > What do I gain with DECAthena as opposed to just running NIS/NFS?

: You get a central administration point for users, file services,
: passwords, etc. You add a user once, then that user can log on anywhere
: without having to update /etc/passwd or automount or anything.
: The administration utility is Motif, but you can use a command line
: if you want.

It would appear that I get most of this with NIS/NFS.  However, from
this and other comments I've gotten, I'm under the impression that, in
addition to better security, I get a lot of nice administrative dodads
that are great for administering a bunch of workstations.

At first blush it would look like I've got a great environment for
DECAthena. I want it so that any of my users can sit down at any
terminal/PC/MAC and connect to any workstation and see exactly the
same working environment. We're using our workstations basically as
a central computing resource for a large number of users, few of whom
are going to make major demands on the systems.  The concept of "the
user's workstation" doesn't really apply.

The drawback to this is that the number of workstations is extremely
low, as in initially 2.  It would appear that DECAthena involves a
certain amount of overhead that's relatively constant regardless of
the number of workstations involved.  This would imply that it's
probably not the way to go unless the expected growth is to some
minimal number of units.  Does this seem like a reasonable analysis of
the situation.

Of course, what I want to do in the long run is load-balancing between
the workstations, but that's another problem.

--
Paul Chapin
User Services Coordinator
Amherst College




