>>The qualifier here is that even a DEC 5000/25 will blow the doors off
>>an Intel chip (even a P6) in floating-point performance.  If you need
>>to do serious number crunching, the RISC workstations are better,
>>hands-down.
>

In defense of Michael, I suspect most of his benchmarks have been done
using free OS's (most notably NetBSD), which uses GCC as the standard
compiler.

Stock GCC 2.X does not do any sort of Pentium optimizations, while I
expect the DEC compilers do a fair job of optimizing the code for the
processor.  That and a lack of a Fortran compiler (g77 was just release,
haven't looked at it myself) also makes much of the existing heavy
number crunching stuff useless.

Given a compiler that could compile pentium-optimized code I suspect his
results would be different, but until pentium optimizations are made
standard in GCC I suspect for most folks a DECstation will outperform
a Pentium box for the general user.

SPEC #'s and other benchmarks are a good starting point, but *real*
applications make for a much better benchmark. :-)



