I did a bit of experimenting this weekend and found the same... the DEClaser 
2200 driver is faster than the 2250 driver.  Not 2-10 times in my case, but it 
printed a page in about 1 minute 30 seconds as opposed to 2 minutes 20 seconds 
for the same page printed using the postscript driver and postscript 
cartridge.

I also discovered what *may* be part of the reason for the huge difference in 
spool file sizes.  The HP driver may be doing some additional optimization 
before writing out the spool file.  I went back through the graphics viewer 
program I wrote... it's dumping a graphical representation of the activation 
values of nodes in a neural network.  Activation values are lines of varying 
orientations centered around a point.  I modified the code so that if the 
activation value were low enough so that the line would be just a point, it 
doesn't even try to draw the line.  That cut the size of the DEClaser print 
files by a factor of at least 20 for a 256x256x12 network layer.  That smaller 
file prints in just over 2 minutes using the 2250 driver and in 1.5 minutes 
with the straight 2200 driver.  (I haven't tried the same huge file on the HP 
yet.)

This *could* explain the problem with my program, but what about MS Project 
prints, Excel Charts, and any Excel spreadsheet that's sized at print time?  
Could these be suffering from the same problem?  Anybody got the source code 
for the printer drivers?

