In article <3mkdv6$kve@hobbit.gandalf.ca>, loiselle@charm.gandalf.ca (Vance Loiselle) writes:
:Distribution:
:
:Thanks for the response. One last question, if a Vax administrator
:were to be used to setting up Decnet networking using routers, and 
:two new networks were added using bridging, say a vax on one net using
:3.1 and another on another net using 3.2, would there be any immediate
:differences on the vax config for this? The vaxes in this case can't see
:each other, but LAT is working great between them.
:Comments?

  Note: With a bridge between the two Ethernet segments, the two nodes are
  effectively on the *same* LAN.

  I'd look for (as was previously mentioned) any DECnet address collisions
  between current and new nodes, and I'd also check on the bridge for any
  address or protocol filtering that might be programmed into it.  (Various
  bridges can be user-programmed to filter various combinations of multicast
  addresses, specific addresses, and specific Ethernet protocols; DECnet is
  one such `filterable' protocol.  When filtering is enabled on a bridge,
  the bridge is quite `opaque' to the specified address(es) or protocol(s).

  You will also want to check the DECnet executor MAX ADDRESS, MAX AREA, and
  MAX ROUTER values on the two hosts -- If you *really* are using 3.1 and
  3.2 and don't have a number of DECnet routers around, this isn't likely
  a problem, though.

  You may also want to consider bringing someone in to look at this Ethernet
  configuration.  (It's rather difficult to troubleshoot Ethernet and DECnet
  problems remotely.  At best, we can guess at potential problem areas.)
  
  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
