Well, not to salt a wound or anything, but I worked in DEC CS for many, many
years, including the US CSC and WW HQ.  From my points of view, the problems
you are discussing are not one of csc specialist training or skillset,
but rather due to the fact that almost all of the management (including
the engineering planning and tools/process development management) is, well,
lets just say that their problems exist between the back of their
seats and their keyboards.  Give most of them a real problem and you are
likely to see it lost and obscured in an endless stream of meetings and 
presentations. 

Of course, you wouldn't be able to bring it to their attention
until you first submitted a request for a process request, and then waited until 
the fourth month of the quarter for a committee of process representitives to
analyze the request for defects, and if you submitted just the right number pages
in just the right number of systems each with an approved sponsor you could 
expect to find out if a process to fix the broken process was already in review. 

I'll bet you think I'm kidding, don't you?

Almost as a whole, they are more concerned with looks than substance, with process
as opposed to results, and with making sure they are seen in the right meetings.

There are a few good people languishing in mgmt, but they have woeful little
impact or resources.  

The vast majority of the specialists are excellent.  The vast majority of
the managers and technical planners are not.

/greg (not speaking for anyone really, and this is just opinion).

