>
>This is a response to David Mathog, mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu, from one of
>my colleagues in the Alpha Workstation Engineering group.
>
>
>>it is possible to 
>>mount the upper RZ29 forward by about a centimeter by using long screws that 
>>came with it, and running them through the ventilation holes in the disk cage,
>>[rather] than the (incorrectly placed) mounting holes.
>
>    The current mounting position of the drive relative to front of the
>    SIMMs, although not ideal, does not require your suggested rework plan
>    in order for customers to install memory after the drive bracket is
>    installed.  The current bracket mounting locations were designed to
>    allow room for a PCB style LED/Reset module which was replaced with a
>    smaller, hard-wired version prior to our first shipment.  An change has
>    been implemented to moved forward the mounting hole locations to
>    allow for greater ease of assembly.  This design change will be phased
>    in on AlphaStation 200 shipments starting in late April [or so].
>

(For those following this discussion, this is all for a 200 4/166 workstation)

It should be noted that the power supply connectors for the upper and lower
drives are different - one is a pass through connector, the second a
regular power connector.  If one attempts to install the regular power
connector into the bottom drive it will not be possible to seat the disk
cage, if the SIMMs are in place, or the SIMMs, if the disk cage is in
place.  However, installing the regular power supply connector on the upper 
drive in the designated position makes it even harder to install the SIMMs
with the disk cage in place, since it extends the 1.5 cm upper disk
overhang even further. 

>>There is significant strain on numerous cables inside this box.
>
>    Your concerns relative to the number of cables and their density are
>    duly noted.  However, we have performed vigorous system level testing on
>    a large number of units over an extended period of time and have not
>    seen one failure relative to your prediction of SIMM warp, shorts, MLB
>    warp, etc.

Stress on components and cables will tend to cause long term failures, not
short term failures (unless the stress is SO extreme that it snaps
connectors loose and so forth).  While I do believe that you tested these
systems extensively, I'm not reassured by those tests that these stresses
will not result in system failures several years from now. 

>    Kenny expounded upon additional mechanical changes which we will be
>    implementing this year on our AlphaStation 200 products.  We appreciate
>    constructive criticism such as yours and we pledge to improve the
>    upgradeability and "in box" experience for our valued customers on
>    future low profile desktop PCI based workstation designs.
>

Since I've used this machine I've confirmed another of my suspicions - the
CDROM drive,  positioned under the diskette, is a pain to work with.  In
order to insert a CDROM the keyboard has to be repositioned - otherwise the
CDROM tray smacks into the back of the keyboard when it opens.  Either
that, or one needs enough space so that there is a 6" gap between the back
of the keyboard and the front of the unit.  But the gap solution isn't
great, since keyboards tend to slide back with use.  So, I'll admit that
this is a minor inconvenience, but it is such an obvious concern that it
should never occur.  Other manufacturers seem to get this right - this is
the first computer I've used where I had to move the keyboard to insert
removable media! 

Another point.  The cord comes straight out of the back of the keyboard,
approximately 5 inches in from the keyboard's left side.  It then runs 
right into the front of the desktop unit.  If the cord were inset only a
little bit, then I would just move the keyboard over to the left so that it
cleared the CPU unit, but moving it 5 inches to the left results in an
awkward offset between the keyboard and the monitor.  This even though the
monitor itself must be placed on the system box off center to the left, so
as not to cover up the top mounted speaker. 

Overall, I'd still have to rate the mechanical design of this machine as
inferior.  That isn't to say that it doesn't work - electronically it is
fine, it's just that it has all of these annoying little mechanical
problems that a workstation just shouldn't have.  Perhaps it just doesn't
make sense to try to pack so much into so small a desktop enclosure. 


Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@seqvax.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



