into comp.unix.ultrix), I have the drive on the DECstation again.
(We remember: I bought a TEAC FC-1 SCSI floppy disk drive)
Well as far as I can tell now the disk drive works fine and it is
only the physical format that is too weird.  At the moment the drive
insists on 32 sectors of 256 bytes each per track instead of the
usual 512 byte sectors.  I can sort of use the one disk I formatted
with the drive while I had it connected to the Atari ST, but still
can't format disks with the DEC.  I can't use the disk for real since --
while the drive likes it and lowlevel accesses work -- putting an ufs
filesystem on it didn't work and won't be of much use either.
Using the mtools results in garbage as every second half of data they
write gets lost (at least it looks like that)...


As the previous owner of the drive tells me that he used it on a
HP machine as-is and used it with 512 byte sectors just fine, I wonder
what HP does differently when addressing the floppy disk drive.

My best guess is that I just have to change some jumper to convince
the drive to use 512 byte sectors again.

Question: Does anyone have jumper details for a TEAC FC-1 disk drive ?

