In article <D6up1r.MnG@world.std.com>, gnewman@world.std.com (Gary
Newman) writes:
>Peter Mardahl (peterm@alfven.eecs.berkeley.edu) wrote:
>
>:   I'm curious what the memory bandwidth is on the various 
>: AlphaStation systems is.  I can find this statistic nowhere:
>: not on the web site, not on anything I've read on this
>: newsgroup, and not on anything I've seen in other sources.
>
>We've measured the 2000/300 (same as AXP150) at about 30 Mbyte/sec
>and the Alphastation 400/233 at about 70 Mbyte/sec.

  Ah, those sound a lot like I/O bus measurements, not memory
  bandwidth numbers.  The 2000/300 uses a EISA bus for I/O that
  has a peak rate of about ~33mb/sec, the 400/233 has a PCI which
  has a peak rate of ~130mb/sec, and I've measured ~65mb/sec
  doing programmed output to it (putimage from X11).

  Memory is typically not connected via the I/O bus, and is
  generally much faster than the I/O path.

