In article <3mjt42$nt1@news.duke.edu>, gallatin@hpdemo.isds.duke.edu
(Andrew Gallatin) writes:
>I was wondering if anybody out there is willing to comment on the
>usability of the ATI Mach64 graphics adaptor on AlphaStation 200/400
>series machines in comparison to the ZLXp-E1 cards.
>
>What kind of a performance hit, if any, does one incur with the ATI?
>What resolution does it run at?  Is it easy/possible to change
>resolutions/frequencies? (on our AlphaStation w/a ZLXp-E1 card, this
>is done via dip switches, I'm assuming this is done in s/w w/the ATI).
>
>I'd love to hear from somebody who has both the ATI and the ZLX card
>who could give me their impressions.
>

How about from one of the engineers who wrote some of the code
for the Mach64 ;-).

The ZLX-E1 is the fastest 2D graphics that you can buy, from anyone.
It's performance scales with the CPU speed, and gets greater than
17 xmarks on the fastest systems.  This is the highest number reported
on any system, on any graphics, from any vendor.  On the 233 MHz
system, this card gets over 14 xmarks, which still makes it as fast
as the fastest graphics from other vendors.  If you need absolute
performance, then this is the card to get.  The X11 server has many
man years of development, and is highly optimized.  As a bonus, it
has low-end 3D primitives and can run OpenGL applications.  It is a
great card for 3D wireframe (with 3D flat shaded vectors greater than
2 million/sec).

The ATI Mach64 card is a top card in the PC space (along with the S3
based cards).  The version that DEC is reselling on the 4/100 is the
low-end 2mb DRAM card (the CX chip).  Its server is based on a design
developed for low-end cards, and has less than a man-year of effort
in the engineering effort specific to the Mach64.  However, the card
still runs very fast for the cost.  The CX card gets ~5 xmarks on
average (depending on the CPU speed it's faster or solwer by perhaps
1 xmark).  And I have clocked the VRAM (GX) version (unofficially)
at 7 xmarks (in 1024x768 mode on a 233 MHz OpenVMS system).  There
are no 3D primitives, and OpenGL & PEX will not work on this card.

The ATI cards resolution can be changed using configuration files
(this is O/S specific) and will run 640x480, 800x600, 1024x768 and
1280x1024 - all at 8-plane 256 color.  Note that the resolution has
a direct impact on performance for DRAM (the ZLXp is VRAM-only).
The higher the resolution, the more bandwidth needed to transfer the
DRAM contents to the RAMDAC, and the less bandwidth available to
the drawing engine to access the DRAM.  So 1024x768 is faster than
1280x1024 on the ATI Mach64.  The ATI card has 1024x768 as the default
so that it will run on all monitors that we sell.

Right now, you *must* use the DRAM card, since there a some bugs in 
the shipping code when using the VRAM cards (I've fixed these in the
next release of OpenVMS).

The ZLXp-E card has switches to allow it to run at many resolutions,
and also allows sync to be output on green - to allow non-multisync
workstation monitors to work.

My personal observations on the performance is that the ATI Mach64
cards (both DRAM and VRAM) are sufficiently fast for a typical X11
workstation.  For typical applications, they are great.  And cheap.
If you are not doing graphics-bound things (and most applications
are not graphics bound BTW) - then buy the 2mb ATI Mach64 CX card.

If you need really fast graphics, the ZLXp can't be beat - it can
get 35-64mb/sec image rates (depending on the CPU speed) and over
2 million 2D and 3D vectors/sec.  So if you're doing CAD, or
animation, or low-end 3D - get a ZLXp-E.

