> I have a couple of questions stemming from my Thesis research that regard
> the Decsystem 10.  I would be much obliged if anyone could enlighten me.
> 
> 
> Thank you,
> Chris Edler
> 
Chris: In case you haven't found it already, there was a pretty comprehensive
article on the architecture and history of the PDP-10/DECsystem-10/DECSYSTEM-20
written by Gordon Bell in the "Communications of the ACM" in the early
eighties. It was part of a special issue on computer architectures.

The short story on the rise and fall of the 10/20: it was derived from the
PDP-6, 36-bit word size, and was one of the first systems to be designed
and shipped with a real live timesharing operating system. There were four
major processor versions: KA-10, KI-10, KL-10, and KS-10. The KI and KL
supported demand paging. The KL was front-ended by a PDP-11/40. The KS-10
(DECSYSTEM-2020) was designed as a "small-office" system, less powerful
than the KL, but with half the footprint of a VAX-11/780. DEC worked on
a couple of follow-on projects. When the "Jupiter" machine had lower
performance than expected, they cancelled the project. When DEC announced 
this at DECUS, the scene was understandably ugly. They sold a total of a
few thousand systems over the life of the product. Of course, aside from the
KS-10 (AKA 2020), these were megabuck systems. An "alt" group was created
recently to discuss 10s and 20s. There was also a pretty comprehensive
table of major/minor variants on the 10 and 20 released (by Joe Smith?)

Hope this helps.
