on system that can be considered to be a kind of "DECsystem-10."


In article <3mhaf0$bti@charnel.ecst.CSUChico.EDU>
cedler@guzzler.ecst.csuchico.edu (Christopher S. Edler) writes:

>I have a couple of questions stemming from my Thesis research that regard
>the Decsystem 10.  I would be much obliged if anyone could enlighten me.

The DECsystem-10 is one family of systems based on the PDP-10 architecture,
defined as those PDP-10s which ran the Tops-10 operating system.  The other
family commercially available from DEC (now calling themselves Digital) is the
DECSYSTEM-20, running the TOPS-20 operating system.  (NB:  The capitalizations
used in this paragraph are those of DEC marketing materials; I'll be reasonable
about them from now on.)  Other operating systems for this architecture include
WAITS (Stanford), ITS (MIT), TYMCOM-X (Tymshare, now part of MCI), and the OS
run by CompuServe.

>Whatever happened to the Decsystem 10?

In 1983, the Large Systems Group was the DEC cash cow, with about 3000 systems
installed world-wide at an average US$1,000,000 per system.  The Large Systems
SIG was the largest sub-organization within DECUS (the Digital Equipment Corp.
User's Society).  A new PDP-10 system, code named Jupiter, was expected from
DEC "Real Soon Now" and life was good.

In a frenzy of "one company, one architecture" DEC cancelled the Jupiter
project and moved the engineers to a VAX project code named Venus (the VAX
8600).  Droves of customers, told that they had to convert applications away
from an architecture that had supported them since 1964 (with the PDP-6), opted
for price/performance rather than vendor loyalty, since the vendor had shown no
loyalty to their customer base.

The sheer size of the Large Systems customer base forced DEC to compromise on
this effort to eliminate a product line.  DEC was forced by market pressure to
agree to support OS development until 1988, and provide hardware support until
1993.

There are a few hundred Dec-10 and Dec-20 systems in the world still, running
applications which companies have decided not to port for various reasons.
There are even people who own small systems which they run in their homes, for
the simple reason that they do some jobs better than anything else.

>What did it evolve into?

It didn't.  It was, and is, successful in its own right.

There are two manufacturers of PDP-10 follow-on systems:  SC Group (originally
Systems Concepts) builds a system that works exactly like a late-model PDP-10
(a KL10B, to use the DEC model number); XKL Systems builds a system that will
execute user-level code but uses modern technology for the backplane bus and
peripheral interfaces.

>Was it successful?

Extremely so.  The PDP-10 was a major cash generator for DEC, at least as
important as the PDP-8 (which was also killed by the move to "one company, one
architecture") and the PDP-11 (which they haven't quite been able to kill off
yet, but they're trying).  It netted more than US$3,000,000,000 (billion, if
you're from the US, thousand million if you're from the UK) in initial sales,
along with hardware and software maintenance contracts and additional
peripherals.

>Does anyone remember its specs?

Depends one which specs you have in mind.

36-bit word, word-addressable.  Bytes are defined by a pointer mechanism,
including both length and position within the word.  ASCII text was represented
as 5 7-bit bytes per word, with a bit left over.

Original address space was 18 bits, 256KW (262,144 words).  Later, an extended
addressing mode was added which provided up to 30 bits of addressing; DEC used
23 bits in their implementations.  (The SC systems have the same limitation;
the XKL TD-1 uses the entire 30-bit address space.)

The KL10B ran slightly more than 1 million instructions per second, not bad for
1974 technology; earlier systems were slower.  Both the SC and XKL boxes are a
good bit faster.

I'd be happy to answer any further questions either here or in private e-mail.
I'll also point out that there is a newsgroup alt.sys.pdp10 which is frequented
by PDP-10 hackers of all stripes, including some old DEC hands, if your site
receives alt.* newsgroups.
