In Article <3mjls4$e23@hobbit.gandalf.ca>
loiselle@charm.gandalf.ca (Vance Loiselle) writes:
>I have a couple of Decnet questions. Any help would be great.
> 
>1) Is DECNET Bridgable?

Everything is bridgable. Bridges don't have any idea about protocols.

>2) Do the AREA.NODE numbers have anything to do with routing/bridging
>   ie Are they anything like tcpip where you must bridge between the
>   same network number and route between different net numbers?

They have a bit to do with routing, but there is no linkage similar to that in
IP.

DECnet phase IV uses a two tiered routing scheme where local routing is done by
routers in the same area and designated "area" routers handle routing to other
areas. Area routers are also local routers, but local routers may not be area
routers.

Normally all nodes on a LAN are in the same area, but there is no requirement
for this. It is normal to have an area that includes many LANs and routers,
though. On the DECnet internet (yes, there is one), an area usually includes
several entire sites, often in different cities. The only requirement is that
all nodes in an area must be able to reach all other nodes in that area without
leaving the area.

If there is a router on a LAN, all initial connect requests are always sent to
it and, if the node being connected to is also on the LAN, the router informs
both nodes and drops out of the interaction. The end nodes cache this and
future connections do not involve the router.

Fully explaining DECnet routing would require a VERY long discussion. You
really need to get the DEC manuals from a VMS doc kit.

