In article <3mjls4$e23@hobbit.gandalf.ca>, loiselle@charm.gandalf.ca (Vance Loiselle) writes:
|I have a couple of Decnet questions. Any help would be great.
| 
|1) Is DECNET Bridgable?

Yup.  (What isn't?)

|2) Do the AREA.NODE numbers have anything to do with routing/bridging

Yup.

|   ie Are they anything like tcpip where you must bridge between the
|   same network number and route between different net numbers?

Something like and something different.

An IP [sub]net must be flat at layer 3 - no routers, only bridges
and below.  By contrast, DECnet allows for arbitrary topology
within an area - a vastly nicer arrangement than IP's.

Areas, however, must be conjoined in a particular topology - 
they must be joined by "area" or "level 2" routers, which must
be connected at layer 2 (the "bridging layer") to each other.
In other words, the "area routers" must be themselves interlinked
for their areas to communicate.

Cheers,

Aaron

