In Article <3mkdv6$kve@hobbit.gandalf.ca>
loiselle@charm.gandalf.ca (Vance Loiselle) writes:

>Thanks for the response. One last question, if a Vax administrator
>were to be used to setting up Decnet networking using routers, and 
>two new networks were added using bridging, say a vax on one net using 3.1
>and another on another net using 3.2, would there be any immediate differences
>on the vax config for this? The vaxes in this case can't see each other,
>but LAT is working great between them.

I don't know that I understand the question, but I'll try.

Merging nets (whether bridged or routed) requires careful planning. There must
be NO conflicts in addresses, especially on a bridged network. As long as there
is only one node 3.1 and one node 3.2 and no other conflicts, and the entire
LAN is in area 3, bridging the nets should work just fine with no changes
anywhere. Of course, if the two nets are routed elsewhere, there is a lot more
to worry about.

There is also the question of which router will be the designated router for
the LAN (controlled by the ROUTER PRIORITY circuit parameter) to provide
efficient routing.

But the end nodes (VAXen not configured as either AREA or ROUTING IV) will not
need any attention, and even routers will likely not need any changes.

