From: "M.A. Jackson" <M.A.Jackson@ncl.ac.uk>
Subject: Re: Using a postscript printer attached to a VXT2000
Date: 1995/04/09
Message-ID: <Pine.SUN.3.91-941213.950409044057.22435M-100000@tuda.ncl.ac.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 100059475
to: "Marcos J.J. Montes" <marcos@embezzle.stanford.edu>
content-type: TEXT/PLAIN; charset=US-ASCII
organization: University of Newcastle upon Tyne
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

On 29 Mar 1995, Marcos J.J. Montes wrote:

> We have a Digital LN03R ScriptPrinter attached to the serial port of a
> Digital VXT2000. We are able to make screen dumps from the VXT to the
> printer. We would like to be able to also print to the printer from other
> machines (like a decstation 3100) that are also attatched to the same
> network that the VXT is attatched to.
> 
> I imageine there is a piece of software that someone has that I am missing.
> Something that would allow the decstation to dump a postscript file 
> directly to the serial port of the vxt2000. Does anyone know what this
> software is, and how I may obtain it?
> 

	Marcos,

	The Software Product Description (SPD 31.34.05) for the VXT says
	"Users can access the local printer [on the back of the VXT 2000]
	from a host system [unix (or VMS)].  Queued LAT printer support and
	TCT/IP lpr [protocol] support is provided."  So it sounds very much
	like you don't need _additional_ software (unless you use VMS and
	DECprint Supervisor DCPS which is optional software).  AFAIK Vers 2.1
	of VXT software includes support for making available an LN03 attached
	to the VXT as a queued, network printer either from a unix lpr print
	queue (or VMS print/queue).

	You mention the LN03R and postscript.  My interpretation of the
	software product description was that the VXT 2000 produced screen
	dumps of graphics on the LN03 using the LN03's sixel graphics
	support, not postscript. i.e. the VXT requires as little as an
	LN03+.  I wouldn't have thought that would stop you from printing
	postscript to it from your DECstations etc. though.

	Your post has only just propagated here, so hopefully you'll have
	got some more authoritative advice by now.  Anyway, good luck.

	MJ.



