this specific case, a quickie Windows viewer I wrote which just creates the 
page and does a lot of MoveTos and LineTos.


>If the graphics is a bitmap, then that's probably exactly what's happening.

I don't believe it's a bitmap.  As I was saying above, in the specific 
instance that's currently causing me grief, the graphic is generated by a 
Windows app. I write it using Visual C++, doing printing pretty much the 
standard way Visual C++ almost writes for you.  I generate the images to the 
printer in the same way I generate them to the screen, using MoveTo and LineTo 
commands... and not *that* many of them, at that.  And I print to both 
printers using the same application, so the same type of graphic should be 
getting sent to both... unless the drivers are translating/handling the 
graphics calls differently.


>Our 2250 is attached to the other end of a 9600 baud line on a terminal
>server. As you can imagine, it takes ages to print a large bitmap from
>Windows NT (or anywhere, really). The same probably goes for yours as well.
>On the other hand, your NT systems might print directly over the network
>to the 4si, resulting in a huge speed increase: the spool file is still
>enormous, but it disappears so fast you just don't see it.
>
>You'd have to give more information about your setup.

Yea, I guess I did leave out a bit.  :-)  Okay, here goes... the DEClaser is 
directly attached to the back of an NT 3.5 Workstation via the Parallel port.  
I print to it using the DEClaser 2250 driver.  The HP is attached to the back 
of our NT 3.5 Server via the Parallel port.  I print to it using the HP 
LaserJet 4si printer driver.  (That tends to make me think communications link 
speed too the printer isn't the bottleneck.  If it were, both setups should 
experience it.)

As for the spool file sizes, printing to the DEClaser generates very large 
spool files and takes several minutes just to dump all the data into the spool 
files.  Printing to the HP doesn't appear to generate huge spool files and 
doesn't take several minutes to either generate the spool files or to dump 
them to the printer.  

Hmm... let's see what else.  The NT Workstation is running on a 486DX2-66 VLB 
system with 32 Mbytes of memory, the server on a P60 PCI system with 64Mbytes.  
That should make some difference, but all the comparisons we've done here 
indicate it's rarely more than a 4-1 difference... certainly not the >100 to 1 
difference we're seeing with the printing.

I can't think, offhand, of any other data that might affect things, other than 
the drivers generating different types of spool files.  For what it's worth, 
we used to have similar problems printing from Windows machines to a DEClaser 
2150 served from our VAXen via Pathworks.  There may be a clue in that 
problem... If we printed an Excel spreadsheet in its default scale, it printed 
quickly.  If we used the printer driver to resize it to some percentage of its 
normal scale, it would take quite a long time to print.  (Just another bit of 
info... perhaps it's relevant, perhaps not.)


Anyway, that's all the relevant information I could think of regarding the 
setup.  Does any of that help?

