It's not entirely true that with SGI you pay for the graphics engine you
don't need. Their Challenge series servers, for instance, do not include a
graphics engine, and I've found them to be competitively priced - especially
when their higher-performance architecture is considered. If you already have
something that you can use as a console, and you don't need the fancy graphics
of an Indy or an Indigo 2, then Challenge becomes an attractive option.
Btw. according to the numbers I saw (and don't ask me what they were; I only
retained the conclusion, the details faded away) a Challenge S with a 150MHz
R4400SC beats the pants off of Sparcstations 10 and 20 in just about anything,
including context switching - which ought to translate into better multi-user
performance.


