jcooley@world.std.com (John Cooley) writes:
>I've always thought it was best for software companies to try to give
>students access to as many of their products as possible.  Everyone benefits
>(as long as no surreptitious commercial chip development is going at the
>university using the software) because the students get to learn cutting
>edge tools and the companies selling these tools get a large body of
>experienced users as a result.  (Sort of like what DEC did with VAXes
>a few years back.)

Mark Zwolinski <mz@ecs.soton.ac.uk> wrote:
>Let's not overdo this altruism thing.   Some manufacturers give away software 
>to universities in the US and as I understand it, they can offset their costs 
>against tax.  In, for example, the UK, the tax laws are different and 
>universities get charged.  Even with 90% discounts, some pieces of software 
>are prohibitively expensive for universities.  In the European Community, the 
>Eurochip scheme has allowed universities to act as a consortium and to 
>purchase ECAD software at, say, $300 per package per seat.  But the 
>manufacturers are not making a loss on anything.  I agree with John's 
>comments above - I just wish more software companies saw things that way.

Mark, what DEC did with VAXes may seem altruistic but it was more like a
carefully crafted long term marketing ploy that had the side effect of
helping universities plus (I'm not sure) getting DEC a nice tax break.

To give you the orders of magnatude costs & sales involved, it cost 
(wild estimate) $10,000 in parts & labor to make a VAX wholesale.  The 
commericial world would pay (estimate) $110,000 for such a VAX and the 
universities would pay (estimate) $17,000 for the same machine.  Instead 
of seeing this $93,000 apparent loss of profit, the people at DEC realized
that there was no way they could have sold $110,000 machines to universities
in the first place -- but they could get an *increadable* new pool of 
experienced VAX users, in the students, each crying to buy more VAXes at 
their new companies once they graduated!

I remember in the news a few years ago that Apple Computer was going to 
try something similar involving MacIntoshes and U.S. high schools but 
couldn't get the U.S. government to give them the tax write-off for it so 
they backed out.
