In article <noer-0804951715150001@mac12.dana2.swarthmore.edu>, noer@cs.swarthmore.edu (Geoff Noer) writes:

Please pass this onto Ross Dickson also at swarthmore, since he
asked the same question and I only want to answer one of them.
I may have answered this by mail.

>
>I am working on setting up a DEC 3000/400 and have a
>question about hard disks.  We have three -- one rz58
>that we were told is "system-rated" and two others,
>rz57s, that we were told are "data-rated".  According to
>the person that we got this system from, it is very
>important that we put /, /usr, etc... on the rz58 and
>not on either of the other two.

The biggest difference between the RZ58 and the RZ57 is that
according to the V3.2 SPD ONLY the RZ58 is supported.  This
means you have to treat the RZ57s with the same level of
respect you would some random 3rd party drive.  Or perhaps
more.  Consider.

As far back as OSF/1 V1.3 the RZ55, RZ56 and RZ58 have been
supported.  But not the RZ57.  This begs the question why it
isn't supported when the disks around it in capacity are.  Two
answer suggest themselves:

1.  It wasn't qualified.  This seems unlikely since the RZ55 and
    RZ56 were.

2.  It failed qualification.  This is more likely, though I
    don't know.  If it failed qualification, then there is
    probably something wrong with it that causes it to fail;
    sometimes.  Perhaps it corrupts data under a heavy load,
    or it corrupts data randomly, or it misbehaves during a
    bus reset, or randomly causes bus reset, or (the litany
    of strange SCSI problems goes on).

Are you willing to trust your data with a disk that we could
have supported but didn't for some reason?  This is the sort
of question that system managers should lose sleep over...

On the other hand, they may have worked fine for the previous
user...

