In article <3mehla$lnn@larch.cc.swarthmore.edu>, ross@raptor.sccs.swarthmore.edu (Ross Dickson) writes:
>
>Also does anyone know what sort of swap space to physical memory ratio works
>best on these machines?

The right one?  The problem is that there isn't one right answer that
works for every configuration.  The amount of swap space you need is
related to how much VIRTUAL memory you need to use and which swap
space allocation algorithm you want to use.

If you use the "eager" swap space allocation method, then you'll need
as much swap space as you want virtual memory.  Period.  Finding out
how much swap space you'll need then becomes an exercise of estimating
the virtual memory needs of the system in something past normal usage.

If you use the "lazy" swap space allocation method, then you'll still
need to estimate how much virtual memory you'll be using, but only
need enough swap space to make up the deficit between the real memory
and virtual memory.

While 2-3 times the amount of physical memory might the right amount
for a "typical" system and work-load, the extremes can make this far
too much or far too little.  You can get Alpha systems with 1 GB of
memory.  At 2-3 times that a lot of swap space.  But if typical memory
use stays below 1 GB you clearly don't need 3 GB of swap space.

On the other hand, if you have a reasonably behaved application that
wants to 1 GB virtual memory per copy and have 128 MB, you'll need a
lot more swap space then 2-3 times.

