OK, I admit it, I was hoping someone would ask that question.

Here's what I heard at the video of the press/customer event yesterday. 
Larry Ellison, president of ORACLE, said that ORACLE running in 32 bit mode
on the AlphaServer 8400 is the second fastest version of ORACLE.  He then
compared it to the fastest, namely ORACLE running in 64 bit mode on the
identical hardware.  For a simple scan of the whole database, 64-bit ORACLE
was 3x faster.  As more complex tests were attempted, the speedup got greater
and greater until it got to 278x for a 5-way join. 

Caution: I have not seen the above results in writing; I am giving you a
from-wetware-memory transcript of a verbal report and 35MM chart.  But I
believe they are accurate, and are actually quite logical and understandable:
when you have 64-bit addressing to 14GB of main memory, there are lots of
operations you can do at memory-speed rather than disk-speed.  That's easily
a factor of 10,000 or more faster for some operations.  And of course the
21164's 300 Mhz quad-issue doesn't hurt!

