In article <3me2ns$san@h20-hrze.uni-duisburg.de>, 
rottland@fb9-ti.uni-duisburg.de (Joerg Rottland) writes...

>Does anyone know the type of memory used in Alphastations 200 4/XXX and 
>Alphastation 250 4/266?
> 
>Could one use standard 72 pin PC SIMM's work or do they have a special design?
>I think they need parity?

    The memory modules used in the AlphaStation 200/250/400 are industry
    standard 72-pin, 36-bit, 70 ns SIMMs.  Because we implement longword
    (32-bit) parity, 33-bit SIMMs can also be used (unused pins in this
    case are 35, 37, and 38).  The obligatory warning about using
    non-qualified parts follows.

    We do extensive qualification of industry-standard SIMMs, and find that
    some vendors' parts fail in our systems;  in every case, these parts
    are also found not to meet the relevant specifications.  It may well be
    that these parts work in some environments that do not require rigorous
    adherance to the specs.  Regardless, we do not sell SIMMs that do not
    pass our qual, nor do we officially support any third-party SIMMs (for
    the same reason).

    If you choose to add unqualified SIMMs, you're on your own.  There's a
    good chance that they will work just fine.

    If it were me, I'd consider what I was using the system for.  If I
    didn't care a lot about data integrity, or sustained up time, I might
    try to save a little money by using parts I can order through COMPUTER
    SHOPPER.  On the other hand, if I was running my business on this
    machine, I'd be very cautious about buying from the low bidder.

    -- Kenny

