This is a response to David Mathog, mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu, from one of
my colleagues in the Alpha Workstation Engineering group.


    David,

    My name is Dan Riccio and I am the responsible engineer for the
    mechanical design of the AlphaStation 200 series enclosure.  First off,
    thank you for taking the time to present your issues in such a clear
    and thorough manner.  The following are my responses to your comments:


>it is possible to 
>mount the upper RZ29 forward by about a centimeter by using long screws that 
>came with it, and running them through the ventilation holes in the disk cage,
>[rather] than the (incorrectly placed) mounting holes.

    The current mounting position of the drive relative to front of the
    SIMMs, although not ideal, does not require your suggested rework plan
    in order for customers to install memory after the drive bracket is
    installed.  The current bracket mounting locations were designed to
    allow room for a PCB style LED/Reset module which was replaced with a
    smaller, hard-wired version prior to our first shipment.  An change has
    been implemented to moved forward the mounting hole locations to
    allow for greater ease of assembly.  This design change will be phased
    in on AlphaStation 200 shipments starting in late April [or so].


>There is significant strain on numerous cables inside this box.

    Your concerns relative to the number of cables and their density are
    duly noted.  However, we have performed vigorous system level testing on
    a large number of units over an extended period of time and have not
    seen one failure relative to your prediction of SIMM warp, shorts, MLB
    warp, etc.  Also, our manufacturing plants are building and shipping
    these systems in volume and their assembly process reflects installing
    the SIMMs last, after the cable dressing is completed.


>Just to top it all off, the manual that came with the system had a table of
>contents, but no page numbers on the pages.

    Our doumentation people tell us that the documentation is demand
    printed, and they were told that only an internal order was supposed to
    have been missing page numbers.  They are already checking with the
    manufacturing site to ensure that this problem is permanently resolved.


    Kenny expounded upon additional mechanical changes which we will be
    implementing this year on our AlphaStation 200 products.  We appreciate
    constructive criticism such as yours and we pledge to improve the
    upgradeability and "in box" experience for our valued customers on
    future low profile desktop PCI based workstation designs.

    Regards,

    Dan

