I'm glad you asked that question!  (In fact, I'm so glad that this is my
second attempt to post the attached; if you do see it twice, my apologies.)

Here's what I heard at the video of the press/customer event Tuesday.
Larry Ellison, president of ORACLE, said that ORACLE running in 64 bit
mode on the AlphaServer 8400 is the fastest version of ORACLE.  He then
compared it to the second fastest, namely ORACLE running in 32 bit mode
on the identical hardware.  For a simple scan of the whole database,
64-bit ORACLE was 3x faster.  As more complex tests were attempted, the
speedup got greater and greater until it got to 278x for a 5-way join. 

Caution: I have not seen the above results in writing; I am giving you a
from-wetware-memory transcript of a verbal report and 35MM chart.  But I
believe they are accurate, and are actually quite logical and understandable:
when you have 64-bit addressing to 14GB of main memory, there are lots of
operations you can do at memory-speed rather than disk-speed.  That's easily
a factor of 10,000 or more faster for some operations.  And of course the
21164's 300 Mhz quad-issue doesn't hurt!

For more official results, here is Tuesday's Performance Flash.  This is
a 132 column document which I have attempted to squeeze into 80 columns
for the convenience of newsreaders.  Pick up the official 132-column
(and longer, Postscript) versions from the usual ftp/www locations after
they are posted...

