organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <GRUNWALD.95Apr11132239@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>, 
grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu (Dirk Grunwald) writes...

>Not so much geared for DB's, but they co-announced 64-bit Oracle at
>the same time, and DB is obviously a big market.
[snip] 
>Given that our sables (2100-4/275's) already out-perform the SGI Power
>Challenge, the turbolaser should give SGI a run for the money until
>the MIPS R10000 comes out, and possibly after that as well.

Hi Dirk,

From the above I would gather that you are saying that TurboLaser isn't
so much oriented towards databases/commercial computing, it's oriented
toward technical computing.  You're wrong. You're right.  I mean... I
mean, it's both.

The 21164 Alpha chip can do quad issue.  Two of those instructions can
be integer, two can be floating point.  So I suppose one could argue
that a scientific program that uses both, versus a commercial program
that uses only integer, will take more advantage of the chip. 
(The fact that scientific programs contain both integer and floating
instructions is probably the reason that a Sable with 202/292
SPECint92/SPECfp92 outperforms a Power Challenge with 109/311.)

But dual-integer-issue certainly doesn't hurt an integer application.  
In Tuesday's performance flash, compare Sable with a single-integer-issue
21064A chip (AlphaServer 2100 4/275) to the new Sable model with a
21164  chip (AlphaServer 2100 5/250) for SPECint92: upgrading the 
server to the new chip moves the SPECint92 rating from 202.9 to 277.1, 
even though the newer 2100 runs at about a 10% slower clock speed.

As has been discussed with some frequency on newsgroups, to really take
advantage of a powerful chip you need to spend $$ on getting the data
and instructions into the chip quickly.  That's a big part of what you
pay for in a large server.  For the scientific codes, the game seems to
usually be to optimize the D-stream; whereas for commerical codes you
are more likely to be I-stream limited (Dick Sites' data).  In either
case having a very fast system bus is a big help for getting data from
main memory into the chip.  TurboLaser's system bus is on the order of
1.5x faster than PowerChallenge's, if I am correctly interpreting the
SGI literature.  (If I am not, please accept my apologies in advance --
it's hard to interpret raw bandwidth ratings.)  Certainly we know that
TurboLaser achieves on the order of 2x more than its predecessor in the
DEC line.

A major point of the commercial performance message is that TurboLaser
lets you have up to 14GB of memory, which commercial applications can
use to turn disk-bound queries into memory-bound queries.  Is memory
capacity as important for technical applications?

Leaving aside hardware for the moment, there are major opportunities for
software to help one exploit a RISC system.  Besides ORACLE's Tuesday
announcement of full support for 64-bit addressing, we also announced
leadership in technical benchmarks:

We published a series of LINPACK 1000x1000 benchmarks that cranked some
amazing MFLOPS ratings, for example 410.5 Mflops achieved on 1 cpu,
2.282 Gigaflops on 8 cpus.  (I hold in my hand a very nice paper from
SGI that proudly declares a 300 Mflops theoretical peak for
PowerChallenge with 1 CPU.)

But vendor code changes are allowed for LINPACK 1000x1000; not all
programmers will be able to hand-code at this level.  So what about the
SPEC benchmarks?  There, hand-coded changes are not allowed (although
enthusiastic use of compiler switches is still allowed).  TurboLaser easily
breaks ***500*** SPECfp92.  This is available today, and no one else
gets over 311 with a shipping product.  

But even without enthusiastic use of compiler switches, the SPECbase
numbers show 444 SPECbase_fp92 and 314 SPECbase_int92.  I sit down the
hallway from the compiler developers, and have seen them do a lot of
work over the last 4 years to generate code that can run at these
levels without requiring software developers to stand on their heads.

Finally, as one more software example, there are system libraries that
have been optimized to take advantage of Alpha, such as the Digital
eXtended Math Library (DXML).  I do not have permission from the DXML
group to quote their recent benchmarks outside the company, but suffice
it to say that the RISC-oriented coding practices done for LINPACK
1000x1000 are similar to the practices used in the DXML group, and DXML
is achieving Mflops ratings that are similar to the LINPACK 1000x1000
rating.  A scientific programmer who calls the DXML routines does not
need to learn all the ins and outs of extracting the best performance
from the chip.

To summarize this note (sorry for the rambling), as one looks at
different components of the AlphaServer 8000, including chip, bandwidth,
memory capacity, and software, there are leadership elements throughout
for *both* commercial and technical application performance. 

    /John Henning
     CSD Performance Group
     Digital Equipment Corporation
     henning@zko.dec.com
     Speaking for myself, not Digital



