Well, I'm not sure if we count as legal to answer your question, but...yup,
we ordered them, we've received them (after a bit of fighting over who got
to stand where in line for the limited allocations of internal systems),
we're running ORACLE benchmarks, as well as lots of others, and the lab is
FFFUUULLLLLL of disks!  (If you build a performance testbed for an IO 
benchmark, and you want to see how big a configuration you can build before
you saturate an incredibly fast 12-way-SMP-CPU, you use an incredibly large
number of disks.)  And yes, Larry Ellison's claims about ORACLE performance
on Turbolaser are technically credible based on what we have seen in our lab
as well as what he has seen in his.

>What do you think?

That I'm really excited to see what's been done with Alpha.  That the new
chip (21164) met its goal of running compatibly with the previous chips,
only faster (e.g. quad issue instead of dual).  That the system designers
for TurboLaser succeeded in their goal to match the faster chip up with a
*much* faster system bus.  That the folks at ORACLE have succeeded in making
their software 64-bit-clean and others will follow. 

That with spring finally come to New Hampshire, and TurboLaser announced
(excuse me, I mean the AlphaServer 8200 and 8400), there's hardly anything 
more that one could need in life.  

Sorry...my enthusiasm ran rampant for a moment there. 

