> Yes folks, if you want to put the disks in a 200 4/166 then you do have to
> take ALL of the SIMMs out.  At that point it goes from impossible to merely
> difficult to get all of the disk cables in place.  A key finding not
> mentioned in the previous post is that it is possible to mount the upper
> RZ29 forward by about a centimeter by using the long screws that came with
> it, and running them through the ventilation holes in the disk cage, rather
> than the (incorrectly placed) mounting holes. This does leave the disk at a
> very slight tilt of about 2 degrees. By doing this mounting position one
> gains reasonable (in this context) access to the forward side of the SIMM
> sockets. Rather than this baroque mounting method it would of course make
> more sense to drill some new mounting holes into the disk cage, but I have
> no doubt that this harmless, but unauthorized, modification would void the
> warranty. 
> 
> So, I've got all of the pieces in the box, but no way I'm happy.  There 
> is significant strain on numerous cables inside this box.  The worst 
> problem is that the wires coming out of the lower disk power connector push
> on the SIMMs with considerable force no matter how one tries to reroute 
> them.  These power connector wires are quite stiff, and the loop that runs
> from the flat lower disk drive connector to the upper, normal, power
> connector acts like a spring pressing on the side of the bank 0 SIMMS. 
> It's anybody's guess what will fail first.  Will the affected SIMM warp
> from the pressure, or just get shoved out of it's socket?  Will one of the
> power wires push through the insulation that separates it from the metal
> SIMM retaining clip, and so short out?  Will the motherboard warp from the
> transmitted pressure? It might take a while for one of these to occur, but
> the mechanical design stinks and I'm predicting that it will lead to system
> failures. 
> 
> Has anybody out there added SIMMs in the third bank in the field? There are
> cables running all over that area (through the space that these SIMMs would
> occupy) and even if these are lifted out of the way, access is really poor.
> I'm wondering if Digital might not be assembling such systems by putting
> in the SIMMs before the wiring! 
> 
> Just to top it all off, the manual that came with the system had a table of
> contents, but no page numbers on the pages.
> 
> These are the kinds of problems one expects to find in cheap PC clones, not
> in a workstation from a reputable manufacturer. 

    Does anyone know if these problems also apply to the minitower models
of the 4/166 and 4/233?

