In article <3lp19e$93k@usenet.rpi.edu>, kaploww@cs.rpi.edu (Wesley Kaplow) writes:

>I have obtained a DECStation 5000 recently.  Unfortunately, the
>system comming to me has no disk.  I have access to another DECStation,
>as well as the appropriate software on CD-ROM as well a a valid license.

With enough extra disk space on the other DECstation, you could run
the new diskless.  I wouldn't, but it could be done.

>The DECStation seems to use SCSI disks.  However, does this machine
>require a special SCSI disk (from DEC) or can I use a standard SCSI
>disk?

Recent versions of ULTRIX are quite happy to use well behaved
SCSI disks.  There was a time, long before SCSI-2 was cast in
firm mud, that some disks weren't well behaved and these might
not have worked.  If the standalone installation system can get
the geometry for the mkfs command to make the root file system,
then it is probably well behaved enough.

Versions of ULTRIX before V4.2 are also a bit suspect in what
SCSI drives they'll support.  In V4.1 and earlier, the driver
was very SCSI-1 like.  V4.2 and later is much more SCSI-2
friendly.

>Also, is there room inside the main box for a disk drive,
>or do I need an external drive?

Depends on the model of DECstation 5000.  I think the Personal
DECstation series (Model 20, 25, 33) may have room for at least
one 3.5" form factor disk inside.  The Model 100 series (120, 125,
133, 150) have room for two.  The Model 200 and 240/260 have to
use external disks.

