In article <3lru0b$rje@cherokee.advtech.uswest.com>,
    crohrer@advtech.uswest.com (Chris Rohrer) writes:
|>Hi DEC specialists,  I'm considering going to alpha 2100s with OSF/1 and
|>several processors.  I am new to the symmetric multiprocessing world and
|>I'd like to know you have to know or worry about in such a system.  Does
|>the user interface change? Are there extensions to the UNIX commands to
|>allow individual user use of each of the processors?  Are processes merely
|>allocated to the least-loaded processor in a totally transparent fashion?

You can let the scheduler deal with additional CPUs in an opaque
manner, or you can set up processor sets and explicitly assign
processes yourself.  We don't use the latter technique on our
system, so I can't give any gory details.  I've included one of the
man pages below, as a starter... Have fun!

Dan Harrington
============================================================================

runon(1)                                                             runon(1)


NAME

  runon - Runs a command on a specific CPU or processor set

SYNOPSIS

  /usr/sbin/runon cpu_num command [argument ...]

  /usr/sbin/runon -p pset_id [-x] command [argument ...]

DESCRIPTION

  The runon utility runs the specified command (command) on the specified CPU
  (cpu_num).  The argument variable specifies any flags or arguments for the
  specified command.  All the threads created by the command inherit the CPU
  binding to the specified CPU (cpu_num), including threads created as the
  result of a fork.

  If the -p flag is used, the specified command is bound to a processor set
  rather than to a specific processor.  Because a processor set can contain
  more than one processor, the -p flag is useful for multi-threaded applica-
  tions.  In addition, you can use the -x flag to specify exclusive access to
  a processor set.

FLAGS

  -p        Binds to a processor set instead of a processor.   You must
            specify the processor set identification number (pset_id), which
            is a unique integer that identifies the processor set and is
            returned by the pset_create command.

  -x        Specifies exclusive use of a processor set.


RELATED INFORMATION

  pset_create, psradm(1), pset_info(1), pset_assign_pid(1),
  pset_assign_cpu(1), processor_sets(4)




