Subject: Re: How does one raise limits in csh under OSF1?
Date: 1995/04/07
Message-ID: <3m3srf$g7f@jac.zko.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 100059463
references: <3m1hl8$as4@azure.acsu.buffalo.edu>
content-type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
x-url: news:3m1hl8$as4@azure.acsu.buffalo.edu
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1b2 (X11; I; OSF1 V2.0 alpha)

This is how I'd do it.

        # mkdir /usr/adm/stanza
        # cd /usr/adm/stanza
        # sysconfig -q proc > proc.stanza

Edit proc.stanza and add a "proc:" label.  Indent the rest so it looks like
this:

proc:
        max-proc-per-user = 64
        max-threads-per-user = 256
        per-proc-stack-size = 2097152
        max-per-proc-stack-size = 33554432
        per-proc-data-size = 134217728
        max-per-proc-data-size = 1073741824
        max-per-proc-address-space = 1073741824
        per-proc-address-space = 1073741824
        autonice = 0
        open-max-soft = 4096
        open-max-hard = 4096
        ncallout = 844
        round-robin-switch-rate = 0
        round_robin_switch_rate = 0
        sched-min-idle = 0
        sched_min_idle = 0
        give-boost = 1
        give_boost = 1


Change the value of max-per-proc-stack-size to what you want (it's in bytes).
Then load the stanza file into the subsystem configuration database.

        # sysconfigdb -a -f proc.stanza proc

Now reboot the system (no kernel build required).

You can keep proc.stanza for future reference, and if you need to make further
changes to proc.stanza, use -u to update the subsystem configuration database,
instead of -a to add.

-- 
John McNulty		      
Digital Equipment Corporation 
CSC/UK Unix Support Group




