From: mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
Subject: Alphastation 200 4/166 - idiotic placement of memory and disks
Date: 1995/04/08
Message-ID: <3m4mmp$lhm@gap.cco.caltech.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 100059459
organization: Biology Division, Caltech, Pasadena, CA USA
reply-to: mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Our spanking new Alphastation 200 4/166 showed up today, with
the extra 32 Mb of memory, 2 RZ26, and an RRD43 loose in boxes.  No
problem, I've installed disks on computers before.

The memory goes in - no problem.
The CDROM goes in - no problem.
Pull the drive carrier - no problem.
The lower drive goes into the carrier- no problem.
The upper drive goes into the carrier, but the screw holes are in a silly
  place and so it sticks back 1.5 cm farther than the lower drive.  There
  doesn't seem to be any other position where all 4 mounting screws can go
  in.  All sorts of drive names are stamped into the metal, but not RZ26. 
  Not a good sign, but at least the drive is in the carrier. 
Put the power and SCSI cables on.  VERY DIFFICULT.  Hardly any room to work
  and everything is all twisted up and tied down to the frame.  Finally I
  get them on. 
Drop the carrier back in and...

Holy shit!  The power cable connector comes down on memory banks 0 and 1
  before the carrier can slide into position.  But the cables can't go
  on after the carrier goes down because the upper drive sticks out over
  the lower drive, so the lower drive can't be touched once the carrier is
  in place!  It looks like there's going to be about 1 nanometer between
  the back of the power connector and the side of the nearest SIMM.

It *might* be possible to take all the memory out and put it in after the
disks, but with all of those cables in there it looks awfully dicey.

So who's been down this path - is there a simple solution, or are we 
talking a trip to the sheet metal shop for a new disk carrier?

Any help will be appreciated.

Thanks,

David Mathog
mathog@seqvax.bio.caltech.edu
Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 



