In article <3li2f3$3jm@gap.cco.caltech.edu> mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu writes:

   >Those with access to MBone technology 
   > etc,

   Oh such joy, the high priests of Unix grant respectability to yet another
   artificially Unix specific technology!

   It isn't like the rest of us even have a shot at this one - the source code
   isn't available, and the perpetrators have not deigned to port it to
   inferior platforms such as VMS, Mac, Windows, WNT, etc.  If this were a
   commercial product that wouldn't be so galling - that would be their
   decision to make. But the source of this code is (please correct me if I'm
   wrong) Lawrence Berkeley Labs, which last time I looked, was running on my
   taxes. 

My goodness. Having a bad day?

The "mbone" is based on IP multicast protocols. IP multicast began in
1989 or so with Steve Deering's PhD at Stanford, and has become fairly
widespread (but not universal) in Unix TCP/IP implementations. Real
support is just now becoming common among the router vendors (see for
example cisco's announcements at Interop this week).

Most Windows TCP/IP stacks don't support IP multicast (yet), which
makes it hard to write windows applications that use it. (I think FTP
Software's does, but I may be confused). (Not to mention Win31/WfW's
shortcomings in terms of accurate timing and direct access to video
memory, which is also a big problem for these applications)

Mac/OS TCP/IP doesn't support IP multicast (yet),which makes it hard
to write Mac applications that use it.

Don't know about VMS, sorry.

Win95 will eventually support IP multicast. WinNT will support it.
Apple's "Open Transport" will support it. Do you suppose applications
will follow shortly?

Applications currently used in the mbone have come from Xerox PARC,
LBL, UMass, Inria (France) and other places. Most, including some from
LBL, have source available, some don't. One reason (which you may or
may not agree with) that some don't is that the author -knows- there
is a problem or limitation in some internal algorithm, and doesn't
want the current version to become widespread.

A number of of commercial products are in development, a few quite
close to completion.

Most of this work has been or is now running through the internet's
"standards" process in the IETF, along with related technology like
audio-video transport protocols, network quality-of-service control
protocols, and session management protocols. Anyone can participate in
this activity follow the work, and FTP the documents.

Finally, the LBL folks, as tax-supported researchers, have a strong
obligation to do research, not program porting. Their results show up
as papers, conference tutorials, and so on, as well as code. 

				regards,
				 -john

