In article <3ll8i2$cn7@gap.cco.caltech.edu>, brown@altair.krl.caltech.edu 
(C. Titus Brown) writes...

>EVERY SINGLE DAMN TEXT FILE has 20 or more lines of text on top saying
>essentially that DEC owns the copyright on this file.  Wonderful.  Why?

Hmmm... I've worked closely with, and liked, two lawyers at Digital. 
One is a lawyer who turned into a programmer, and the other is a
programmer who turned into a lawyer.  Both have amazingly precise minds
-- say what you mean when you speak with them, not some random handwaves.  

It was the programmer-turned-into-lawyer who explained to me that
software is how I feed my family; and companies have been known to
*lose* all rights to their software by careless actions!  She said that
it is essential to protect against the "innocent trespass" defense: The
system said "Welcome", so I thought it was inviting me in; I didn't see
any signs saying "do not copy" so I thought I could re-use this and
adapt it to my needs; the files weren't protected so I thought they were
for everybody.

Meanwhile, she listened carefully as I explained to her that a certain
draft copyright policy was technical nonsense, as it said that EVERY
piece of software was supposed to display a copyright notice on the
screen.  How would you like to wait for every called layer of the X
library to say "Hi, I'm X, and I am copyrighted" every time it gets
invoked?  She also arranged for me to give a presentation to other
members of the legal staff to help them understand the different kinds
of software.  And they apparently scrapped the requirement to post
notices on the screen for every piece of software.

Meanwhile, back to the copyright in the source files - yes, it's due to
the lawyers, but at least one of them was not a technical idiot.  And
since I *am* a legal idiot, I choose to believe her that the copyright
needs to be redundantly inserted in (almost) every file.

    /John Henning
