Subject: 21164 scheduling question
Date: 1995/04/03
Message-ID: <DAVIDM.95Apr2205230@piston.cs.arizona.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 100717476
organization: The University of Arizona, Tucson, AZ
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch

In section "Coding Guidelines" of chapter "Scheduling and Issuing
Rules for the Alpha 21164" of the 21164 manual, there is an example of
code that executes faster if a NOP is introduced (this chapter is
available as a postscript file on gatekeeper.dec.com but I forgot the
exact name of the file).  The example is:

---
Code example showing bad ordering	Code example showing good ordering

(1) [a]	LDL	R2,0(R1)		(1) [e]	LDL	R2,0(R1)
(3) [b] ADDL	R2,R3,R4		(1) [f] NOP
(4) [c] ADDL	R2,R5,R6		(3) [g] ADDL	R2,R3,R4
(5) [d] ADDL	R4,R8,R9		(3) [h] ADDL	R2,R5,R6
					(4) [i] ADDL	R4,R8,R9

  NOTES: The instruction examples are assumed to begin on an INT16 alignment.
	 (n) = Expected execute cycles
---

What I don't understand is why [d] issues only in cycle 5 (instead of
4).  According to the "Instruction Latencies" table, ADDL has a
latency of 1, so the result of [b] should be available in cycle 4.  Is
there another reason for the split-issue of instructions [c] and [d]?

BTW: Is the full 21164 hw ref manual available anywhere?  A
postscript file would be preferable, but hardcopy is OK, too.

	--david
