Well, without saying which dealer gave us this price, but its a special deal
for the campus for a limited period, and is as good as it will go I think:
(meaning >25% below standard list price)

Alpha Station 4/100
Configuration: 32Mb Memory, 535Mb internal hard disk, ATI Mach64 8 plane
graphics card, 17" colour monitor, power cord, keyboard, mouse and THREE YEARS
on-site warranty. 

At a price of about 3950 pounds sterling without tax (17.5% payable for all
except medical research) (call it 1.55 US$ to the pound, that makes it about
US$6,000 at discount and without taxes) that seems a tad expensive compared
with a *minimal configured* Pentium. And a lot more than the minimal Multia
which is about 2,500 pounds for starters here, (that will be 24MB memory,
probably 350 MB hard disk, 15 inch monitor) without discounts or tax. 

Now I *know* we are comparing apples with oranges here, but the point I was
making (trying to) was that the Multia is not competing really with the low end
workstation, but with the high end PC, and that is a very different and much
larger market in which WNT will be the OS of choice, but that would allow
Digital to create a cheaper unix and VMS system by gaining on the expected
economies of scale if they so choose. Whether that suits the marketing
department I don't know, but I can see good value in getting 2 to 3 Multias
running VMS or OSF/1 in place of one AlphaStation in enviroments where I want
those OS's and more machines for the same price. 

I guess what it comes down to is that its not the Multia is necessarily the
right machine at the right price, but that we want cheaper machines... :)) and
no doubt that will be the case whatever the price!

