>I have obtained a DECStation 5000 recently.  Unfortunately, the
>system comming to me has no disk.  I have access to another DECStation,
>as well as the appropriate software on CD-ROM as well a a valid license.
>The DECStation seems to use SCSI disks.  However, does this machine
>require a special SCSI disk (from DEC) or can I use a standard SCSI
>disk?  Also, is there room inside the main box for a disk drive,
>or do I need an external drive?

There are various models of the DECstation 5000.  The -100 (-125, -133, -150)
series can accomodate up to 2 internal 3.5" HH disks, and 1 front loading
device (like a CDROM, tape drive, floppy).  The -200 (-200, -240, -260) series
require all external storage.

I don't think that any of the systems are particularly picky about the drives. 
It's going to be more of an operating system issue, anyway.

