X-Deja-AN: 100178966
references: <D5Ft1p.F5K@world.std.com> <1995Mar15.084912.22@thehulk> <1995Mar27.175727.30640@oxvaxd> <3leigo$35j@jac.zko.dec.com> <3levn0$6nq@news.duke.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


    I've tried to gather real data regarding the many AlphaStation 200
    issues raised in this string.  Questions and comments have been
    paraphrased below.

    Thanks to all of you who have participated in this conversation, both
    on the Internet and in Digital's internal network.  We do care about
    your experiences, good and bad, with our products, and we try to fix
    the problems we hear about when we can.

    -- Kenny


    q:	Just what *is* the memory limit, anyway?

    a:	There are several answers ...

    	1) 192 MBytes, using currently qualified SIMMs;

    	2) 384 MBytes, using 64 MByte SIMMs (Digital will qualify these when
        their cost drops to a point where they become attractive, until
        then, you're on your own);

    	3) 512 MBytes is the limit of addressability in the backup cache tag
        rams, so even if real big SIMMs were to come along, you could not
        exceed this limit.

    	It bears repeating here that not all memory SIMMs out there meet
        industry standards.  Digital resells only components that have
        passed our rigorous qualification process.  There are many SIMMs
        that have failed this test, and analysis shows that they all out of
        spec.  That said, the system is designed to use industry standard,
        70 ns, 72-pin SIMMs.  Since we provide longword parity protection,
        we can use either 33- or 36-bit SIMMs.


    q:	But can I fit the bigger, 64 MByte SIMMs in the system?

    a:	Not yet, there's a mechanical interference between the taller SIMM
        and the disk drives.  We are developing another drive bracket that
        moves the drives forward to alleviate this problem.  Future models
        in this family will have the SIMM sockets moved toward the rear of
        the enclosure.


    q:	I'm having trouble accessing any of your SIMMs.

    a:	It is hard to get fingers into all the spots that need poking and
        prodding, without disassembling some of the components (power
        cables, Ethernet Media Adapter Unit [MAU], audio card, drive
        bracket).  We are able to insert and extract SIMMs without major
        surgery, though, by using whatever tool is at hand (pencil,
        screwdriver) to release the retaining clips, and by careful guiding
        of the SIMMs to/from the socket.  Design changes mentioned above
        will help.  The User Information Guide says to remove the drive
        bracket, but our manufacturing personnel put the SIMMs in last, and
        take 'em out first.


    q:	It's hard to install a disk drive.

    a:	There are some design changes coming that will make this somewhat
        easier.  Along with having the SIMMS moved back, the mounting holes
        for the drive bracket are being enlarged.

    	You may find it's easier to remove any memory SIMMs before
        trying to get the drive bracket out.  Disconnect the drive power
        and signal cables, then install the drive.  It's generally easier
        to plug in the cables before dropping the drive bracket back in. 
        Finally, replace the SIMMs.


    q:	The top cover is really hard to put on.

    a:	I hate to say that this gets easier with practice, since you (the
        customer) aren't looking to make a career out of getting in and out
        of this box.  Design changes are being made to improve the fit and
        installability of the cover.

